1. Pod Not Starting / Stuck in Pending State
What is a Pod Pending State?
A Pod in the Pending state means Kubernetes has accepted the Pod, but it hasn’t been scheduled to a node yet. This usually happens because the cluster doesn’t have enough resources, the node doesn’t meet the scheduling requirements, or storage isn’t ready.

Quick Tip: A Pending pod isn’t failing — it is simply waiting for the required conditions before it can start.

Common Symptoms
You may notice one or more of the following:

Pod status remains Pending
No container is created
No application logs are generated
Deployment rollout is stuck
Events show scheduling failures


How to Fix the Problem
Check and adjust CPU and memory requests/limits.
Ensure worker nodes are Ready and have sufficient resources.
Fix scheduling issues such as taints, tolerations, nodeSelector, and affinity rules.
Resolve image pull issues or correct invalid image names.
Resolve PVC or storage-related issues if applicable.


