2. CrashLoopBackOff
Symptoms
The pod keeps restarting repeatedly.
The restart count keeps increasing.
The application never reaches the Ready state.
Common Causes
The application crashes during startup.
Required environment variables are missing or incorrect.
The application cannot connect to the database or another external service.
The startup command or entrypoint is incorrect.
The liveness probe keeps failing, causing Kubernetes to restart the container.
The container is terminated due to an Out of Memory (OOMKilled) error.
How to Diagnose
Start by checking the application logs:

kubectl logs <pod>
If the container has already restarted, check the logs from the previous instance:

kubectl logs <pod> --previous
Then inspect the pod events to identify the exact reason for the restarts:

kubectl describe pod <pod>
How to Fix It
Check application logs and fix the root cause.
Fix incorrect liveness and readiness probe configurations.
Increase memory or CPU limits if the container is OOMKilled.
Verify ConfigMaps, Secrets, and environment variables.
Roll back to the previous working application version if the issue started after a deployment.

