4. Service Not Accessible
What is a Service Not Accessible Issue?
A Service Not Accessible issue occurs when an application running inside a Pod cannot be reached through its Kubernetes Service. The problem may be due to an incorrect Service configuration, missing endpoints, network restrictions, or an issue with the LoadBalancer.

Quick Tip:
If the Service has no Endpoints, it usually means the Service selector doesn’t match any running Pods.
If the Endpoints exist, check the network path (NetworkPolicy, firewall, or LoadBalancer).

Symptoms
Application cannot be reached.
Service is inaccessible from inside or outside the cluster.
Requests to the Service fail or time out.
Service exists, but traffic is not reaching the Pods.
Example

NAME         TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      PORT(S)
web-service  ClusterIP   10.96.120.15    80/TCP
Common Causes
Incorrect Service selector.
No matching Pod labels.
Missing Endpoints.
NetworkPolicy blocking traffic.
Firewall restrictions.
LoadBalancer or Ingress configuration issue.
How to Diagnose
1. Check the Service
kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
Verify:

Service type
ClusterIP
Port
TargetPort
2. Check Service Endpoints
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

## Expected output:
NAME          ENDPOINTS
web-service   10.244.1.15:8080
If the output shows:

<none>
the Service isn’t selecting any Pods.

3. Describe the Service
kubectl describe svc <service-name> -n <namespace>
Verify:

Selector
Ports
TargetPort
Events
4. Verify Pod Labels
kubectl get pods --show-labels
Ensure the Pod labels match the Service selector.

Example: Service

selector:
  app: nginx
Pod

labels:
  app: nginx
5. Check Network Policies
kubectl get networkpolicy
Ensure no NetworkPolicy is blocking incoming traffic.

6. Verify Firewall / LoadBalancer
For NodePort or LoadBalancer Services:

Check firewall rules.
Verify the external IP is assigned.
Confirm the cloud LoadBalancer is healthy.
How to Fix It
Ensure the Pods are running and Service Endpoints exist.
Verify the Service selector matches the Pod labels.
Check the Service type (ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, or Ingress).
Update or fix NetworkPolicy rules.
Verify firewall or security group settings.
Check cloud provider or LoadBalancer configuration for external access.
